  volume   =  13,
  number   =  2,
  pages    = "204--216",
  abstract = "The ability to measure gene expression on a genome-wide scale is
              one of the most promising accomplishments in molecular biology.
              Microarrays, the technology that first permitted this, were
              riddled with problems due to unwanted sources of variability. Many
              of these problems are now mitigated, after a decade's worth of
              statistical methodology development. The recently developed RNA
              sequencing (RNA-seq) technology has generated much excitement in
              part due to claims of reduced variability in comparison to
              microarrays. However, we show that RNA-seq data demonstrate
              unwanted and obscuring variability similar to what was first
              observed in microarrays. In particular, we find guanine-cytosine
